  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FIRC- &apos;Self Reliance 4 Female Inmates&apos;</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Female Inmates &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Returned Citizens Multi-Purpose Cooperative Society (FIRC) is a rightsholder under a previous project implemented by Carmelite Prisoners &#x2018;Interest Organization (CAPIO), funded by Voice in Nigeria. The project supported the coordination and empowerment of female inmates &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; returned female citizens as a group, for economic empowerment. The project identified that within most prison facilities in Nigeria, access to hygienic menstrual materials is unavailable for women and for returned citizens, economic opportunities (after serving their term) are denied due to discrimination &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; profiling. Thus, the project established a cost-effective pad-making workshop within the Suleja custodial center. Through this collaboration between returned citizens&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; serving inmates, the latter now have access to menstrual materials while returned citizens were able to set up their own business where these cost-effective pads are produced &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; sold to the public. Current project aims at upscaling this beautiful conceptin 3 other correctional facilities in Nigeria, but now with returned citizens leading the project as opposed to CAPIO.

The main goal of the current project is to facilitate the rehabilitation and re-integration process for returned female citizens (through skills acquisition) and healthy prison experience for incarcerated female prisoners. More women and girls in the custodial centre will have access to sanitary pads. This will reduce sexual and reproductive health problems caused by poor sanitary practices. The intervention will provide members not only with the skills to produce sanitary pads but also with project and business management skills. Also, theproject will provide a platform to amplify the voice and of the rightsholders and open doors ofengagement with key state actors regarding the change they want to see within the correctional facilities..

Key activities which will help achieve the goal of the project are as follows:
- Advocacy visit to Controller General of Correctional and State Controllers to strategically engage them to take ownership of the process for the sustainability of the intervention in the correction institutions.
- Training of trainers (our members) from Enugu, Lajos and Plateau by our members in Abuja workshop centre
- The trainees will step down the training for female inmates in their respective states
- Establishment of training workshop centre in the three selected states for female ex-inmates

The change that this project hopes to see is reduced stigmatization against the rightsholders, which will impact positively on their self-worth. It is also hoped that the project will facilitate not only life-sustaining skills but will also facilitate the emergence of the Female Inmates &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Returned Citizens associationas a strong group that can change the narrative against incarcerated women and girls in Nigeria.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FEMALE INMATES AND RETURNED CITIZEN</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Female Prisoners &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt; Returned Citizens Co-operative society is an informal group that emerged from a previous intervention under Voice program.  The goal of the co-operative society is toprovide some level of legal recognition to the association. The association is a mutual beneficial platform for both female prisoners and female returned (formerly incarcerated) citizens to provide socio-economic support to one another. The concept came as a result of the stigmatization and discrimination their members experience both within and outside the prison walls.

&#x2022; Many members within the prison lost support of their families and well-wishers due to their social status;
&#x2022; Those outside struggle to find their place including re-integration into the society &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt; getting employment or any means of livelihood when they come out.

The co-operative/association is therefore, their own way to taking chargeof their lives and future. The association was narrowed down to only women due to the peculiar nature of the gender which is also reflected in the level of hardship, exploitation &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt;discrimination both within and outside the prison wall.

Under this platform, they hope to open doors of economic opportunities &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt; social inclusion for their members, devoid of discrimination,criticism &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt; judgement. It will be a lifeline for those coming out of prison to get engaged &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt; re-integrated into the society; and a social support for those still under incarceration.

The co-operative was established by 10members of the association. They lead and implement their activities with the support of Carmelite Prisoners Interest Organization. The association is now recognized by the Nigerian Prison Services under the latter&#x2019;s stakeholders&#x2019; forum.

FIRC upholds the values of mutual respect,empowerment &lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;(&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;&lt;(&gt;&lt;&lt;)&gt;)&gt;)&gt; inclusion.</narrative>
